11.5  Disk encryption: LUKS and the TPM

TL;DR
  - LUKS encrypts a whole partition: without the key, a stolen disk or laptop
    gives nothing. Once unlocked, files are readable as usual.
  - Your passphrase doesn't encrypt the data directly: it unlocks a random
    master key stored in the LUKS header, in one of several key slots.
  - A TPM can hold an unlock key and release it only if the boot chain is
    unchanged: no passphrase at boot, still protected if the disk is removed.

      lsblk -f                          # crypto_LUKS partitions
      sudo cryptsetup luksDump /dev/nvme0n1p3     # key slots, cipher
      sudo systemd-cryptenroll --tpm2-device=auto /dev/nvme0n1p3

On your machine
  Trusted   3 certificate authorities, in /etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt
  AES       aes-aesni, with the CPU's AES instructions · SHA-256: sha256-ni
  RNG       hardware generator tpm-rng-0 feeds the kernel
  TPM       version 2: can hold keys and seal disk keys

  nvme0n1         476.9 GiB  SSD · Samsung SSD 980 PRO 500GB
    nvme0n1p1     512.0 MiB  vfat   /boot/efi   256.0 MiB free (50%)
    nvme0n1p2     476.4 GiB  btrfs  / (+2)      120.0 GiB free (30%)
    nvme0n1p10      1.0 MiB
  sda              30.0 GiB  rotational · removable · USB DISK 3.0
    (whole disk)             exfat  /media/usb
  zram0             8.0 GiB  SSD · swap
  2 loop or empty devices not shown

Pitfalls
  - Damage to the LUKS header loses everything: keep a copy elsewhere
    (cryptsetup luksHeaderBackup).
  - Keep a recovery passphrase or key: a firmware update or Secure Boot change
    makes the TPM refuse, and asks for it.
  - Encryption protects a machine that's off. While it runs, files are as
    readable as on any disk.
  - Changing a passphrase is instant (another key slot); changing the master key
    means re-encrypting the disk.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with atfq 11.5 --why

Related
  Commands  cryptsetup, systemd-cryptenroll, lsblk
  Paths     /etc/crypttab
  Hub       atfq disk Disk
